Generates the C++ data header for the javelin engine, emitting `const` arrays for the type table and construct metadata. Strokes are pre-encoded to key masks (no parsing at load); entry rows are omitted — the javelin-ext walk reconstructs them.

`emit_data_header(types, constructs) -> Result<String, ExpandError>` produces the complete header: PREAMBLE, type rows, per-construct backing arrays (base masks, template ops, fill order), the CONSTRUCTS table, and MAX_OUTLINE_LENGTH. Returns an error if any stroke fails to encode via `stroke_mask`.

Each construct gets three static arrays: `c{i}Base[]` (base-segment key masks, omitted if empty), `c{i}Ops[]` (template ops rendered via `op_initializer`), and `c{i}Fill[]` (fill order indices, omitted if empty). The CONSTRUCTS row references these and includes base length, shape mask (or 0 if no shape), op count, and slot count.

`op_initializer(op)` renders a single `GenOp` with designated initializer for the union: `{.text = "..."}` for TEXT, `{.landing = n}` for LANDING, empty `{}` for SLOT and NEWLINE. Template ops come from `template_ops(template)`, which is called fresh for each construct.

The PREAMBLE struct definitions are load-bearing: GenOp's union saves 4 bytes per op by sharing TEXT and LANDING storage; this is critical — op arrays are the largest flash contribution, and the image must fit under STENO_CONFIG_BLOCK_ADDRESS.